libhsakmt fmm.c (follow-on to va-reuse-defer.patch, gfx803): restore the
defer window's mapping-alive promise that the hsa-runtime's free path
defeats.

WHY
---
va-reuse-defer.patch parks a freed object on a bounded FIFO ("the parked
object keeps its BOs ALIVE (mapping + content intact) for the whole
window") so a lagging access to the freed buffer is tolerated the way
stock gets it from near-instant VA+phys reuse. That promise is broken on
every line that carries the defer: the hsa-runtime's KfdDriver::FreeMemory
calls hsaKmtUnmapMemoryToGPU BEFORE hsaKmtFreeMemory, so by the time the
free reaches __fmm_release's park branch the GPU page-table entries are
already gone (mapping_count == 0). The park then withholds the VA from the
free list (good -- that is the gfx803 miscompute fix) but the object is
reserved-but-unmapped. Any GPU access to a parked VA -- a lagging in-flight
reader, or the late tail of a chunked copy -- now faults with
"Page not present" instead of being tolerated, because stock's tolerance
(recycle the VA and let the new owner remap it before the next touch) is
exactly what the withholding removes.

Reproduced as a hard, repeatable GPU page fault (write from TC, page in
free VA space just past the most recently freed block) with a torch
`x.double().cpu()` alloc/free churn loop on BOTH the 7.14 and 10.0 lines
(same torch 2.13 source; 6.4.4, which recycles VAs immediately, does not
fault and is data-correct). The write that faults is a legitimate late
access of the class the defer is explicitly designed to tolerate -- not an
OOB kernel -- so the correct fix is to make the defer actually keep the
mapping alive, not to hunt the (correct) kernel. Isolated to the fmm with
a minimal HIP repro: allocate + write a buffer, hipFree it, then write it
again from a kernel; with the park unmapped this faults, with the remap
below it writes the parked object's still-resident pages.

WHAT
---
In __fmm_release's park branch, re-establish the GPU mapping the hsa
runtime already removed, guarded on mapping_count == 0 (only when the
unmap actually happened; a second release of an already-parked object is
already a no-op via the deferred flag, and a still-mapped object needs no
remap). _fmm_map_to_gpu is the same path the allocator used to create the
mapping; it re-issues MAP_MEMORY_TO_GPU for each handle and sets
mapping_count = 1. The KFD driver flushes the GPU TLB on map, so the
re-established mapping is immediately visible. fmm_do_release() still
tears the mapping down for real when the object ages out of the FIFO, so
nothing leaks: a parked object occupies its VRAM and its VA exactly as the
defer always intended.

This is gfx803-family-specific only in that the defer itself exists for
gfx803; the unmap-first is generic hsa-runtime code, so the fix is a
strictly stronger version of the existing patch's intent on every line.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (gfx803 box, 2026-08-30): spliced into a 10.0
image -- parktest (write a freed-but-parked VA) now passes 2/2 (previously
faulted 2/2: the parked object's GPU mapping is genuinely re-established
and its content intact).
diff --git a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
--- a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
+++ b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
@@ -209,6 +209,10 @@
 };
 
 static void fmm_defer_release(HsaKFDContext *ctx, manageable_aperture_t *aperture, vm_object_t *object);
+static HSAKMT_STATUS _fmm_map_to_gpu(HsaKFDContext *ctx,
+			manageable_aperture_t *aperture,
+			void *address, uint64_t size, vm_object_t *obj,
+			uint32_t *nodes_to_map, uint32_t nodes_array_size);
 
 /* A freed allocation whose VA is being withheld from reuse for a short while.
  * The KV object is kept in the aperture tree (so the area stays reserved and
@@ -2425,5 +2429,19 @@
 		 * lagging in-flight readers from faulting.
 		 */
 		object->deferred = true;
+		/* KfdDriver::FreeMemory (the hsa-runtime) calls
+		 * hsaKmtUnmapMemoryToGPU before it calls hsaKmtFreeMemory, so
+		 * by the time a free reaches the defer here its GPU mapping is
+		 * already gone (mapping_count == 0, PTEs removed). Re-establish
+		 * it: the defer window's whole point is to keep the VA mapped so
+		 * a lagging access to the freed buffer is tolerated (stock gets
+		 * the same tolerance from near-instant VA+phys reuse), and a
+		 * parked-but-unmapped VA turns any such access into a hard page
+		 * fault. fmm_do_release() tears the mapping down for real when
+		 * the object ages out of the window.
+		 */
+		if (object->mapping_count == 0)
+			_fmm_map_to_gpu(ctx, aperture, object->start,
+					object->size, object, NULL, 0);
 		fmm_defer_release(ctx, aperture, object);
 	}
